## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Turn Off the Lights, Please.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist peers in following multi-step school procedures. Communication: Relay key points from the teacher's lectures to absent students.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist classmates in following conservation steps with visuals (e.g., shows a picture of a light switch and says, "Turn off the lights."). Can relay key points about conservation, using basic words and gestures (e.g., "We save water." and mimes turning off a faucet). Can summarize conservation actions for peers, using simple words and visuals (e.g., pointing to a tree picture and saying, "Save trees, don't waste paper.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the mediation task context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Conservation Charades." Teacher mimes simple conservation actions (e.g., turning off a light switch, closing a faucet, recycling a bottle). Students guess the action using target vocabulary like "Turn off the lights!" or "Save water!" Teacher encourages full sentences and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a conservation action using a visual and simple words. Teacher holds up a picture of a light switch. "Look! This is a light switch. When we leave the room, we say: 'Turn off the lights! Let's save energy.' This helps the environment. It is *easy*." Teacher models pronunciation of /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When do we turn off the lights?" (Expected: "When we leave the room" or "At night.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why do we turn off the lights?" (Expected: "To save energy" or "For the environment.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!" Reviewing conservation actions to prepare for mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Teacher presents a simple "Conservation Tips" infographic or a short animated video that summarizes key conservation actions discussed in previous lessons (e.g., "Use natural light," "Turn off the faucet," "Recycle paper," "Plant a tree"). The content reinforces imperatives and introduces comparatives/superlatives in simple contexts (e.g., "Saving water is *easier* than fixing a leak.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Giving and following suggested actions: "Use natural light when possible." "Turn off appliances when they are not needed." Sociolinguistic: Comparing actions: Using comparatives and superlatives to discuss conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students answer simple true/false questions based on the infographic/video in pairs. The teacher reads a statement, and students show "True" or "False" with hand signals. Examples:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We should leave the faucet running." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Using solar power is *safer* for the environment." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Turning off lights is *the most important* way to save *electricity*." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation. Practicing explaining and summarizing conservation information using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Imperatives ("Turn off the lights," "Let's save energy"), Comparatives ("easier than," "safer than"), Superlatives ("the most important"). Vocabulary: shade, light, water, energy, environment, electricity, turn off, save, recycle, help, reduce, cool, easy, meaningful, bright, safe, easier, most. Pronunciation: /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off." Phonemic awareness: -er in "safer."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Conservation Sentence Builders:</b> Students receive sentence strips with gaps and a word bank. They complete sentences describing conservation actions. (e.g., "Please, ______ off the lights when you leave the room." "Saving ______ is ______ than recycling bottles."). This builds their ability to formulate explanations.
                        <br><i>Teacher action: Circulates, provides assistance with vocabulary and grammar, and checks for correct usage.</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Explain the Picture" Relay:</b> In small groups, students take turns picking a flashcard with a conservation image (e.g., a faucet, a tree, a light bulb). They must explain the action to their group using an imperative and adding a simple benefit or comparison. (e.g., "This is a faucet. Please, *turn off* the *faucet* to *save water*. It's *easy* and *meaningful*.").
                        <br><i>Teacher action: Monitors group discussions, prompting for more detailed explanations and correct pronunciation (/t/ in "turn off"). Encourages use of comparatives ("*easier* than").</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Conservation Coaches:</b> Students work in pairs. One student acts as a "Conservation Coach" and explains three important conservation steps from their previous lessons/materials to their "classmate" (the partner). The coach uses visuals or gestures and focuses on using imperatives and target vocabulary. The "classmate" asks one clarifying question. Then they switch roles.
                        <br><i>Teacher action: Facilitates pair work, ensuring students are actively mediating information, using words like "save energy" and "recycle," and practicing phrases like "I prefer saving energy because it is beneficial."</i></li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!" centered on the "Conservation vs. Recycling Poster" project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> "Poster Explanation to a Visitor."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their project groups (for the "Conservation vs. Recycling Poster"). Each group will imagine a new student or a school visitor is coming to see their poster. Their task is to act as "guides" and *explain* a section of their "Conservation vs. Recycling Poster" to another group (the "visitors"). They must highlight at least two conservation actions, *summarize* their benefits, and *compare* them using comparatives and superlatives. They should use visuals from their poster to assist their explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups finalize their poster (if not already done).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher assigns groups to "visit" another group's poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Host" groups explain their poster section. They must use imperatives ("*Turn off* the *lights*!"), comparatives ("Saving *water* is *easier than* *recycling*."), and superlatives ("This is *the most important* thing."). They should also use gestures and point to visuals on their poster to *assist* understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Visitor" groups listen and can ask one clarifying question about a conservation action or comparison.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups rotate roles or move to another poster.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Teacher action: Circulates, observes group interactions, encourages full sentences, provides scaffolding for vocabulary, and notes effective mediation strategies. Reminds students to use natural light, save energy, and recycle to complete the project focus.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of mediation skills and project completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note student performance on mediation skills during the "Poster Explanation" activity (e.g., assisted peers, relayed key points, summarized effectively, used visuals/gestures). Peers provide brief feedback on clarity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group selects one member to *summarize* the most important comparison from their "Conservation vs. Recycling Poster" project to the class. They must use at least one imperative, one comparative, and one superlative. (e.g., "On our poster, we say: '*Turn off* the *lights*! It's *easier than* making *solar power* at *night*. It's *the most important* way to *save electricity* every *morning* and *night*!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Includes nouns (light, water, energy), verbs (turn off, save, recycle), adjectives (easy, safe), comparatives (easier, safer), and superlatives (most).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and organization:</b> Explanation is clear, simple, and easy for peers to understand.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and assistance:</b> Demonstrated ability to assist peers and relay information effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Correct use of imperatives, comparatives, and superlatives.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, it was *easier* for me to explain _________________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I helped my classmates understand _________________ by _________________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the learning process and preparing for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is *the most important* thing you learned about explaining conservation to others?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use your mediation skills to *help* others *save energy* at school and at home?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate information about conservation? (e.g., visuals, group work, explicit modeling of comparatives/superlatives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to better assist peers or relay key points in future mediation tasks? (e.g., more sentence frames, specific roles for mediation, additional practice with phonemic awareness).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: This lesson concludes the "Turn Off the Lights, Please." theme and the "Conservation vs. Recycling Poster" project. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and a new scenario, building on the communicative skills developed here.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>